Reconnaissance Practice
Introduction

Reconnaissance is the information gathering phase of a security assessment. It is used to collect publicly available and technical information about a target before deeper testing begins. The goal is to understand the target’s external exposure, identify assets, discover technologies in use, and detect potential attack surfaces. This phase helps security professionals build an accurate picture of the organization’s digital footprint.

Reconnaissance can be passive, where information is collected without directly interacting with the target, or active, where tools query services or systems. For this task, publicly available tools such as search engines and internet intelligence platforms are used.

Tools Used
Shodan

Shodan is a search engine that indexes internet-connected devices and exposed services. It helps identify publicly accessible servers, open ports, banners, SSL certificates, webcams, routers, and other devices.

Common use cases:

Discover exposed remote access services like SSH or RDP
Identify web servers and versions
Find outdated or misconfigured systems
View geographic exposure of assets

Example findings:

Port 22 open for SSH
Port 443 open for HTTPS
Web server banner showing software version
WHOIS

WHOIS is used to gather domain registration information. It helps identify ownership and administrative details related to a domain.

Typical WHOIS data includes:

Registrar name
Domain creation date
Expiration date
Name servers
Registrant organization (if public)
Administrative contacts (sometimes masked)

Usefulness:

Understand domain history
Identify associated infrastructure
Verify legitimacy of domains
Subdomain Finder

Subdomain enumeration tools are used to identify subdomains associated with the main domain.

Examples:

blog.example.com
mail.example.com
dev.example.com
api.example.com

Why it matters:

Many organizations secure the main website but overlook older or development subdomains. These can expose login panels, staging environments, test servers, or forgotten applications.

Common tools:

Sublist3r
Amass
Google Search

Search engines can reveal publicly indexed information that may not be obvious from the homepage.

Examples:

Public PDFs containing employee names
Login portals
Old webpages
Exposed directories
Cached content
Job listings showing technologies used

Advanced search operators can refine results, such as searching for PDFs, admin pages, or specific site content.

Information Collected During Reconnaissance
Domain Information

The following details are commonly collected:

Main domain name
Registrar
Domain age
DNS records
Name servers
SSL certificate details

Why it matters:

Older domains may have legacy systems, while DNS records can reveal mail services or third-party providers.

Subdomains

Discovered subdomains expand the visible attack surface.

Examples:

mail.company.com
support.company.com
dev.company.com

Risk considerations:

Unpatched software on forgotten systems
Default credentials on test panels
Sensitive data on internal portals accidentally exposed
Exposed Ports

Open ports indicate services listening on a server.

Examples:

Port	Service
22	SSH
80	HTTP
443	HTTPS
21	FTP
3389	RDP

Security relevance:

Unnecessary open ports increase attack surface. Older services may also contain known vulnerabilities.

Technologies Used

Reconnaissance can identify technologies running on a target website or server.

Examples:

Web server: Apache / Nginx
CMS: WordPress
Framework: React / Laravel
Analytics tools
CDN providers
Programming language hints

Why it matters:

Knowing the technology stack helps defenders understand exposure and patch requirements.

Example Reconnaissance Workflow
Perform WHOIS lookup on target domain
Enumerate subdomains using a subdomain finder
Search Shodan for exposed ports and services
Use Google search to identify indexed assets
Record findings in a report
Real-Life Example

During reconnaissance of a sample domain, the following findings were discovered:

WHOIS showed domain active for 8 years
Subdomain finder revealed dev.example.com
Shodan identified port 22 and 443 open
Google indexed a public employee PDF
Website technology indicated WordPress CMS

This information could help prioritize security review of development systems and publicly exposed services.

Conclusion

Reconnaissance is a critical first step in security testing. By using tools such as Shodan, WHOIS, subdomain finders, and search engines, analysts can identify external assets, exposed services, technologies, and potential weak points. Proper reconnaissance improves later testing phases and helps organizations understand their public attack surface.
